  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 148 AM MDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nRidging over Texas will begin to shift westward into New Mexico on \nSunday, which will start to disrupt the subtropical moisture plume. \nThe weakening of flow aloft will actually help moisture increase in \nthe western half of the state, with PWATs rising back above the 75th \npercentile. Sunday could have the most widespread storm coverage of \nany of the next 7 days as a result. Storm motions will trend slower, \nwith locally heavy rainfall favoring the high terrain of northern \nNew Mexico. A gradual drying trend is forecast into Monday and \nTuesday as heights rise and an elongated ridge blocks off sub-\ntropical moisture from pushing northward into the desert southwest. \nThis pattern will likely continue into mid-week, but confidence \nremains low with what happens beyond that. \n\nSome models are showing anomalous troughing over the Intermountain \nWest transitioning into a reverse monsoon pattern with the monsoon \nhigh setting up over the Great Basin and leaving eastern NM \nvulnerable to the passage of a backdoor front or two. Others keep \nridging over the spine of the Rockies, with moisture steadily \nreturning from the south. The current forecast shows scattered \nshowers and storms over the western and northern high terrain, but \nthis is subject to change drier or wetter depending on how models \ntrend in the coming days.",
